\viewkind4\uc1 \f1\fs20\b WORKSHEET\b0\par
\f1\fs32\b Like rain upon the grass\b0\fs24\par
\f1\fs20 Torah: Deuteronomy 32:1\u8211?52 | Haftarah: Hosea 14:2\u8211?10; Joel 2:15\u8211?27 | Gospel: Matthew 18:21\u8211?35\par
\f0\fs22\i Moses\u8217? last song begins with the Word coming down like rain and sets the unchanging Rock against a heart that, in abundance, forgets from whom it received everything. Shabbat Shuvah (Hosea, Joel) calls to return \u8212? not to self-made fruit but to its Source \u8212? and Matthew 18 shows where that forgetting or returning becomes visible: in how you treat the other.\i0\par
\par\f0\fs24 
\par\b\fs28 How to use this sheet\b0\fs24\par


\par Work from top to bottom: first observe (A1), then weigh (A2), then diagnose (A3), and only then summarise (A4). The direction \u8212? receiving or forgetting \u8212? is the horizon, not the starting point.\par


\par\b\fs28 Basic data\b0\fs24\par


\par Parashah / scope: Ha'azinu \u8212? Deuteronomy 32:1\u8211?52. Haftarah (Shabbat Shuvah): Hosea 14:2\u8211?10 + Joel 2:15\u8211?27. Flanking reading and status: Matthew 18:21\u8211?35 \u8212? New Testament reflection, used confessionally (not as peshat). Audience: study group / congregation.\par



